```toml
[versions]
# existing
kotlin = "2.3.10"
javaVersion = "21"
# new
compose-multiplatform = "1.10.1"
kotlinx-coroutines = "1.10.2"

[libraries]
# existing
kotlinx-serialization-json = ...
ktlint-cli = ...
# new — add whatever the client needs, e.g.
kotlinx-coroutines-core = { module = "org.jetbrains.kotlinx:kotlinx-coroutines-core", version.ref = "kotlinx-coroutines" }

[plugins]
# existing
kotlin-jvm = ...
# new
kotlin-multiplatform = { id = "org.jetbrains.kotlin.multiplatform", version.ref = "kotlin" }
kotlin-plugin-serialization = ...   # already present
compose-multiplatform = { id = "org.jetbrains.compose", version.ref = "compose-multiplatform" }
compose-compiler = { id = "org.jetbrains.kotlin.plugin.compose", version.ref = "kotlin" }
```

**`build.gradle.kts`** (root) — add the new KMP and Compose plugin aliases with `apply false`:
```kotlin
alias(libs.plugins.kotlin.multiplatform) apply false
alias(libs.plugins.compose.multiplatform) apply false
alias(libs.plugins.compose.compiler) apply false
```
This is the same pattern used for the Spring plugins already there.

**`client/build.gradle.kts`** — the new file (see full syntax below).

**`ktlint.gradle`** — no changes needed. It already applies to `allprojects`, so `client` gets it automatically.

### 2c. The `webMain` Intermediate Source Set Trick

The `webMain` source set is an **intermediate source set** that sits between `commonMain` and both `jsMain` and `wasmJsMain`. It lets JS and Wasm share code that is web-specific but not common to all platforms (e.g., browser DOM APIs, `ComposeViewport`).

The configuration syntax in `client/build.gradle.kts`:

```kotlin
kotlin {
    js { browser() }
    wasmJs { browser() }

    sourceSets {
        val webMain by creating {
            dependsOn(commonMain.get())
        }
        jsMain {
            dependsOn(webMain)
        }
        wasmJsMain {
            dependsOn(webMain)
        }
    }
}
```

Key points:
- `webMain` is a **custom** source set — it is not auto-created by the Kotlin Gradle plugin. You create it with `by creating`.
- It explicitly `dependsOn(commonMain.get())` to be in the dependency chain.
- Both `jsMain` and `wasmJsMain` call `dependsOn(webMain)` to inherit from it.
- The convention plugin example references `webMain.dependencies { }` directly because the convention plugin *already sets up* this source set. In a direct `build.gradle.kts`, you must create it manually first.
- Source files go in `client/src/webMain/kotlin/`.

### 2d. Whether Convention Plugin is Needed

No. Convention plugin is not needed. The full `client/build.gradle.kts` can be written directly.

---

## Complete `client/build.gradle.kts` (Recommended Approach)

This is the direct approach — no `buildSrc`, no `convention-plugin-multiplatform`:

```kotlin
plugins {
    alias(libs.plugins.kotlin.multiplatform)
    alias(libs.plugins.compose.multiplatform)
    alias(libs.plugins.compose.compiler)
}

kotlin {
    jvmToolchain(libs.versions.javaVersion.get().toInt())

    jvm()
    js { browser() }
    wasmJs { browser() }
    iosArm64()
    iosSimulatorArm64()

    sourceSets {
        val webMain by creating {
            dependsOn(commonMain.get())
        }
        jsMain {
            dependsOn(webMain)
        }
        wasmJsMain {
            dependsOn(webMain)
        }

        commonMain.dependencies {
            implementation(libs.kotlinx.coroutines.core)
            // add other shared deps here
        }

        jvmMain.dependencies {
            // desktop-specific deps
        }

        iosMain.dependencies {
            // iOS-specific deps
        }

        webMain.dependencies {
            // shared JS+Wasm deps
        }
    }
}
```

---

## Summary Table

| Question | Answer |
|---|---|
| Convention plugin needed? | No. One KMP module does not justify the overhead. |
| Files to change | `settings.gradle.kts`, `gradle/libs.versions.toml`, `build.gradle.kts` (root), create `client/build.gradle.kts` |
| `webMain` creation | `val webMain by creating { dependsOn(commonMain.get()) }` then both `jsMain` and `wasmJsMain` call `dependsOn(webMain)` |
| `ktlint.gradle` | No change — `allprojects` already covers the new module |
| Android target | Excluded — the existing project has no AGP, adding Android to `client` would require adding AGP to the root, which is a separate decision |

